<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a stack-buffer-overflow in the `decode_ise` function in the file `astcenc_integer_sequence.cpp`.

**Analysis:**
The `decode_ise` function declares a fixed-size array `results` on the stack with a size of 68. The function then proceeds to loop up to `character_count` times, writing to the `results` array in each iteration. The fuzz driver can provide a `character_count` value up to 256. In the crashing test case, `character_count` is 76. When the loop variable `i` reaches 68, the write to `results[i]` goes out of bounds, causing the crash.

**Suggestion:**
The `results` array should be dynamically allocated based on the `character_count` parameter to avoid the buffer overflow. Alternatively, if there is a hard limit on the number of characters that can be decoded, the function should validate the `character_count` parameter and return an error if it exceeds the supported size. Given the context of the surrounding code, it seems more likely that dynamic allocation is the intended solution.
</crash-analysis>

